demy-id "q-129405" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{3}$ along the path $y=x$” is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{3\\cancel{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e982015-e57d-5a0b-a9cf-5d08deab7ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448356} {:answer/id #uuid "2459a454-5301-5e77-b21b-b6196bab14e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448357} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d95f94d-2076-5218-bb4b-c6ab553eb89a" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449218} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b6df36c-4778-5e0d-8a29-4c2efc4da127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448358} {:answer/id #uuid "51ef316d-e1c9-5b72-9ff3-7956a1c2c951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448359}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448358} :answer-field/id #uuid "b396f2c8-dc63-5102-8ab9-0b273481f509" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449219} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23411fdc-77e6-57da-b87b-f1f0a95f66cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448360} {:answer/id #uuid "ff37351c-4fe5-5bcc-ab1b-336319013805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448361} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0e78afc-fd6f-5a29-a353-29016479df8c" :answer-field/key "The limit does not exist" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "88abe9a3-7bd6-5ff9-8f3e-e809f8b5b178" :question/math-academy-id "q-129406" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{2(0)(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=0$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2x(0)}{3({0}^{2})-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $\\frac{2}{3}$ along the path $y=x$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x(x)}{3{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2xy}{3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "810c6a8f-0958-52c6-b20f-d96a37359b47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448362} {:answer/id #uuid "08cfa10d-b846-5e6d-bf35-9404324b2a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448363}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448362} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5e3b0a-5393-557f-b53b-8a481a907ef0" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449221} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88619661-430d-5de8-b84b-ec40f974019f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448364} {:answer/id #uuid "1a45e285-3045-5c04-8840-34af5a94d221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448365} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cee3926-16f6-5e9e-9417-558226515e81" :answer-field/key "The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449222} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e8e63f-6e04-57b4-8a7d-297ae9238284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448366} {:answer/id #uuid "6ec1f619-5c35-5d30-81b4-076f333cd284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448367}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448367} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1158047-7645-528c-878f-9126eb72416b" :answer-field/key "The limit does not exist" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7bd92abc-0899-50b8-83be-62e32b920d95" :question/math-academy-id "q-129408" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{0}}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $4$ along the path $y={x}^{4}$” is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{4},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{4}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{{x}^{4}}}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{4})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{8}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}(1+{x}^{4})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2}{1+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $x=0$” is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,x=0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}\\frac{2({0}^{2})\\sqrt{y}}{{0}^{4}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{y\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The limit does not exist” is true. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265823 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "053d7683-eb2b-524a-8f48-34c58516b514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263046} {:answer/id #uuid "107f9a00-2ab2-5ddd-bcac-9802763a9723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263152} {:answer/id #uuid "680b9d63-4091-5ae1-8e3f-46a7c9d43e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263897} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe4251a-b496-5eb8-8465-e83d73fba9d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264944} {:answer/id #uuid "ff129e04-8125-5957-a2fe-9be8a804d26a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264944} :answer-field/id #uuid "afefeac9-9210-5ff6-a466-d566f40851cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "54ba14c7-96f4-538f-92f9-9aee3ad220a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-130096" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1),$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,0) & =\\frac{2(-1)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{2(0)}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\  & =(-1)i+0\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-0⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fa62eab-b90e-5a48-abaf-f85a91f5acd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263290} {:answer/id #uuid "62b1fd5a-785c-5c0d-b89b-dc81b75e19f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263851} {:answer/id #uuid "a52310bc-e27c-5255-928c-7b85e9ff51e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264412} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1bcd1d-dd9d-5585-a7f9-07a54fc0555d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264626} {:answer/id #uuid "f2dab8b3-8a9a-56d3-9061-2a98e26196d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265134}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265134} :answer-field/id #uuid "b246b7e5-0c53-5a83-ad21-caca0e7a4743" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265561}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a7c798f0-d19a-5ddc-9430-afe4c5122532" :question/math-academy-id "q-130104" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2}$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-4xy+5{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-4y)\\,i+(10y-4x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =(2(-1)-4(-1))\\,i+(10(-1)-4(-1))\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨2,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),\\,y-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y+1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab7ecfb-6837-5f43-a7dc-5de26787b651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263102} {:answer/id #uuid "23c6bb01-4817-5934-b31c-f94122a313f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263311} {:answer/id #uuid "4d97af51-08fe-5c7f-90df-c14d7845e35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263672} {:answer/id #uuid "96b21983-40a1-5c92-8a77-84386b07a32d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-i-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264290} {:answer/id #uuid "eae56032-740f-556c-b1d0-0bf3c280086c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263102} :answer-field/id #uuid "86808a14-0099-527e-bba9-8dfc049cb102" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1e97b736-408e-55e1-a895-d40d15156508" :question/math-academy-id "q-130198" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)={f}_{y}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)i-{f}_{x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)j \\\\  & =(-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)j \\\\ t(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))j \\\\  & =i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265853 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db8855f-407d-543c-b41c-01ec8863c250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+{e}^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263273} {:answer/id #uuid "310e7ac2-6457-50a9-b85b-62fe72227f50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-e\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263418} {:answer/id #uuid "313c22eb-142a-5e0a-bbf7-3e694214c7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263420} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef9f166-57a7-581f-b069-feefd3a089a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263557} {:answer/id #uuid "66d4bf27-1916-5b14-ad54-71713514a6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263418} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0fb381c-a4a1-50c1-8a16-52f23963275a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b37d7050-f209-536a-923e-f6a34554696d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130200" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}=e-y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1} & =e-y \\\\ x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=e.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,0)={f}_{y}(1,0)i-{f}_{x}(1,0)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+y)j \\\\  & =(2xy{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}+1)i-{e}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,j \\\\ t(1,0) & =(2(1)(0){e}^{0+1}+1)i-{e}^{0+1}\\,j \\\\  & =i-e\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-e\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265855 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be39072-497d-5f31-85ed-080e23888640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263654} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a31d3c-8503-58a7-8ecf-53ce88e8429e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264681} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f465b6-bac3-55a1-8d75-c1289cf7313e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264733} {:answer/id #uuid "d297311d-d396-508d-a6bd-3e8b3fd8c863" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-3,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264836} {:answer/id #uuid "e32b090c-8a44-582a-8e53-682c5da583b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,-12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264972}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264681} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2ab0ddd-26ef-5f3a-b559-a55d7b1fb92f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1ac4e615-d461-5edb-9d78-97997efce275" :question/math-academy-id "q-130223" :question/problem "At which point on the curve ${x}^{2}y=-8x-4$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}y+8x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+8x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-4.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+8x)\\,j \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy+8)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j,$ we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy+8)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -(2xy+8) & =0 \\\\ xy+4 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{4}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-\\frac{4}{x}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =-8x-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}\\left(-\\frac{4}{x}\\right) & =-8x-4 \\\\ -4x & =-8x-4 \\\\ 4x & =-4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $x=-1,$ we get $y=4,$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1441a2d6-6646-53cf-910b-640be6a3ce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263189} {:answer/id #uuid "1d83c4d7-e29e-518f-8d81-fc678900ebe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263270} {:answer/id #uuid "3151187a-5967-53d5-ae4b-7ac589aab55a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263421} {:answer/id #uuid "5d332831-6792-53d3-9b0e-7703fcddae9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263808} {:answer/id #uuid "970e8c1f-d7a6-5f66-9d87-f248bc2ef3cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i+j)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264293}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263189} :answer-field/id #uuid "2376d46b-16ff-5b21-93b2-796a7646d0b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3aa96b9c-802f-5254-a50f-7fd477588b2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130271" :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,4).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(1,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x}+2\\sqrt{y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,4) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(1,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,4)}{‖∇f(1,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)}{‖\\frac{1}{2}(i+j)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a96a6f2b-900e-5634-9638-19c6ba6bffea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439527} {:answer/id #uuid "5d1afda0-1dac-5620-821a-ad4de506b094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439528}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439527} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a971e33-0a26-5898-ba8a-788195a8a8e8" :answer-field/key "$z=-5$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440121} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2790ee6-862d-5820-b3be-e055f4659069" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439529} {:answer/id #uuid "d99a0961-46ae-512d-9fb7-6207f8fa1ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439530}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439529} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b2d90b2-34d7-5699-a083-f922b6d5712c" :answer-field/key "$z=-2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440122} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41f67dcd-39a1-5085-8fe2-b6379e6ec19b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439531} {:answer/id #uuid "18bcc087-c79b-57e0-b261-df33539990d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439532}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186439531} :answer-field/id #uuid "4559e4f3-c21d-572f-9a65-3dba0baea77f