all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}x & 2x & 3 \\\\ 5 & x & 7 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =x\\begin{vmatrix}x & 7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2x\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}5 & x \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x(x-14)-2x(5-21)+3(10-3x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-14x+32x+30-9x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9x+30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $22,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9x+30 & =22 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x+8 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{{9}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 8}}{2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm \\sqrt{49}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9\\pm 7}{2} \\\\  & =-8,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-8$ and $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265943 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08612547-b005-5392-af1a-df7b474c183f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263076} {:answer/id #uuid "44c75865-eba7-5ea5-899b-6ea970d46b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263604} {:answer/id #uuid "540dadca-dfc9-5c4a-a4a7-eb33cde6b83b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263725} {:answer/id #uuid "73317ca7-7b96-518c-af47-8c1a296a0e5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263999} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9e8555-066d-500b-b643-2c20d52ada5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265217}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263076} :answer-field/id #uuid "f52b9c18-1a55-5825-8634-6431340dd06d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7e9e0a1f-e62f-537b-9ae4-39884aaf945c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130620" :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $x$ given that the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ x & -1 & -x\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ x & -1 & -x\\end{vmatrix} & =3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -x\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ x & -x\\end{vmatrix}+8\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ x & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(-x+2)-5(-x-2x)+8(-1-x) \\\\  & =-3x+6+15x-8-8x \\\\  & =4x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21390a7b-0a54-5e9a-9011-c7deff139c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\frac{5}{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263297} {:answer/id #uuid "91d75f72-1a96-5644-a529-caf7355ab1bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Any value of $p$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264249} {:answer/id #uuid "970c8319-2067-5d80-a590-021621a09030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-3$ and $3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264292} {:answer/id #uuid "f080186a-d1c4-58fa-9d1f-db014dd38fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265109} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1c675c-a294-5ee2-a8ca-549d874e48c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$-10$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264249} :answer-field/id #uuid "a69f31fe-6848-54a7-8e0e-0e1258ee7ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fee93468-4926-51f6-9486-b49c687c7f2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130626" :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $p$ given that the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3p & 4 \\\\ -2 & 6p & -8 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3p & 4 \\\\ -2 & 6p & -8 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}6p & -8 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-(-3p)\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 6p \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(6p-8)+3p(-2+8)+4(2-6p) \\\\  & =6p-8+18p+8-24p \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, this matrix is singular for all values of $p.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "076ccae2-d102-5ad8-b1c2-ac28978e3e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263068} {:answer/id #uuid "1b3882de-2108-52bf-b5e1-4c3304b9a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y-2=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263257} {:answer/id #uuid "a00ce4f7-3888-5dba-a972-b715615f1421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2a0770-0459-500c-aa32-352ae1667e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+3y-6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264813} {:answer/id #uuid "d493ac59-d9ae-5896-bd9e-006d111d56b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+y-7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264362} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7e101e-fe81-5db4-a578-c892744685d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c80428ab-daca-506e-aca7-d4e40b0d604c" :question/math-academy-id "q-130718" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=6.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(3,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i-6y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(3,1) & =2(3)\\,i-6(1)\\,j \\\\  & =6\\,i-6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨6,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨6,-6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{6} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-6)} \\\\ -6(x-3) & =6(y-1) \\\\ -(x-3) & =y-1 \\\\ -x+3 & =y-1 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263005} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9853cb-1b4d-5d99-b740-f207d47a13c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-3y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263384} {:answer/id #uuid "62cb3b0d-c5e0-5140-9b14-0b73eecbc9ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263854} {:answer/id #uuid "70e32100-046e-5e0e-b9f2-eac7fe211b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263976} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d5900d-9aee-5b56-aa97-4e95d9c8f10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264575}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263005} :answer-field/id #uuid "263638a6-9832-58e0-a5f7-da0d13d29e2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d42d4bf9-4eef-57b6-b4ed-d5448bfe2b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-130723" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4xy+2{x}^{2}=x+2y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4xy+2{x}^{2} & =x+2y \\\\ 4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4y+4x-1)\\,i+(4x-2)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(4(-1)+4(2)-1)\\,i+(4(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨3,6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{6} \\\\ 6(x-2) & =3(y+1) \\\\ 2(x-2) & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-4 & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064b3549-4bf4-5a5c-b8dc-3968806ac3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+2y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263057} {:answer/id #uuid "1572d019-302d-55d4-a933-653804f7c50e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263201} {:answer/id #uuid "3a49bd46-cd0b-56cc-ad48-5f7f88b79995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263521} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab79447-463d-53f5-9910-346ef01f273b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3x+y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264187} {:answer/id #uuid "d106df35-59e0-5715-a37e-1c3233721325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x-y=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263201} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca1f3923-ed50-5a12-bc40-f829c5055015" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a95a9cd4-1a5a-56cf-b216-e3ff43ec1b14" :question/math-academy-id "q-130724" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=2y-x-2.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =2y-x-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(0,2) & =(\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}+1)i+(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}-2)j \\\\  & =i-j \\\\  & =⟨1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,2)=⟨1,-1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,2).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{-1} \\\\ x & =2-y \\\\ x+y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266047 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050f1921-3c44-5bbf-b7ea-35eab2c32786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263045} {:answer/id #uuid "6abc818b-9a7d-590c-806b-551c7bbe6fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263916} {:answer/id #uuid "a6997c32-93ac-521d-9b24-002782474a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264426} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0b44e3-00a5-5e34-b4f1-766b6565f684" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,i-j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264496} {:answer/id #uuid "e957c96d-da16-5583-8c41-1dbbf28e3ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265029}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263916} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a084402-00ad-5a44-a106-2a26851c80b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7d357ddc-9cee-5e05-b7e3-0ba7beb5d8f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-131115" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz},$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,4,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz}+)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xy}+\\sqrt{yz})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{xy}}\\,i+(\\frac{x}{2\\sqrt{xy}}+\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{yz}})j+\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{yz}}\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,4,1) & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}\\,i+(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{1(4)}}+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}})j+\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{4(1)}}\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,4,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266050 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4345e16b-0df8-59b8-ad37-650db4ac210e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,3,-5⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263593} {:answer/id #uuid "58520017-6d75-5711-9c4c-279ff100c302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨-2,5,-3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263761} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8b5e1d-96ce-502e-ac9c-14b8cdee3daf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264220} {:answer/id #uuid "a89fe459-90af-559f-b732-4d06764bcb9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264441} {:answer/id #uuid "e315f8f8-9238-56c6-b08f-3a589b1dff97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨4,5,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264441} :answer-field/id #uuid "c90461b1-1149-54d3-9d43-9942adac1495" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5b9d3e0c-64e0-51a4-97be-e5302f43c4c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-131126" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x=5$ at the point $(1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=5.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)k \\\\  & =(2xy+{z}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+2zx)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(2(1)(-1)+(2)^{2})\\text{ }i+((1)^{2}+2(-1)(2))\\text{ }j+((-1)^{2}+2(2)(1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,∇f(1,-1,2)=⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266051 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fcf330d-a9bc-5cf2-b434-a4434b6c918f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,-1,1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263401} {:answer/id #uuid "402f1ba3-23be-5592-8f2a-ad27c9a309bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263569} {:answer/id #uuid "49bbfeb3-58d2-5e76-8091-57f95d4f4d99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263633} {:answer/id #uuid "5b15af4c-f9dd-5de5-aecf-f59227dc3406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263785} {:answer/id #uuid "f1dfc3ea-bfe3-57da-b45d-efee09d91a84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,1,-1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265125}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263569} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a93c22f-f072-55e4-a7e9-f76359ecbdf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265404}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "23ceddb8-6aa1-5bf0-9ebf-c92f08b6f440" :question/math-academy-id "q-131127" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ at the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)k \\\\  & =(2xy{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2xz)i+({x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-1)j+(2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2})k \\\\ ∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =(2(-1)\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(-1)(1))i+((-1)^{2}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-1)^{2})k \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+2)i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-1)j+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-1)k \\\\  & =2\\,i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266052 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f2935d-4872-5cd6-8e24-e225510a7567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2=y+1=1-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263034} {:answer/id #uuid "18adff3c-3a0d-5f5a-815a-c37ea3624b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2-x=y+1=1-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263235} {:answer/id #uuid "36aacf8e-1fcb-56d4-af5e-18ee04eb5d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263484} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbdaf92-bed5-5318-8e56-f73024adfe3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263790} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6dd442-aa88-5bce-a949-069d0d4b49f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-2=y+1=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263235} :answer-field/id #uuid "db9794d6-ba5f-5c97-afad-c0cc73023f27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ec2f8828-cf4a-59b7-bf21-9952b961e912" :question/math-academy-id "q-131128" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}yz=-4$ at the point $(2,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz)k \\\\  & =2xyz\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}z\\text{ }j+{x}^{2}y\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,1) & =2(2)(-1)(1)\\text{ }i+(2)^{2}(1)\\text{ }j+(2)^{2}(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+4\\,j-4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-4)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-4)} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ 2-x=y+1=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266053 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00dfea87-0564-54b8-b2ba-b85b9b81f898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{4}=2-y=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263009} {:answer/id #uuid "02bdc94f-aecb-598c-8c8e-fd84d09e16d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-1=y-2=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263024} {:answer/id #uuid "2143561b-a016-53c4-935f-4521d8cb87c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{y-2}{2}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263298} {:answer/id #uuid "71e1bc5e-929a-5149-b69e-6390e2a09556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=z-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263985} {:answer/id #uuid "cd91307b-95b0-5c36-965f-60e3ce8f9695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264798}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264798} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c8f2342-92e1-5c88-9098-f4db5aaee8bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "46c7ac33-5b38-5319-85fa-4f19aa20d07b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131130" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $x={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ at the point $(3,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(3,2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =-i+2y\\,j-2z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(3,2,1) & =-i+2(2)\\,j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25bb17e4-258b-51bf-91d3-7e60cc8a5b8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266482} {:answer/id #uuid "3b886ea8-78ac-5785-a126-630c13002539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266668} {:answer/id #uuid "90ef5240-d3e8-56bc-a359-94af08fe97cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267433} {:answer/id #uuid "d1666f3f-d78f-5b47-bf2e-cbd0ba0f6a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268015} {:answer/id #uuid "d92bf854-b63d-5ad5-821d-d64b45e82fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267433} :answer-field/id #uuid "c37a1b95-6721-5ce6-92db-42984b088eeb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1798a630-b3d2-5681-be69-eeaa1d664d97" :question/math-academy-id "q-132253" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(3,-1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple and chain rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =y\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}{({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})}^{-1/2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}) & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\cdot (2x+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,-1,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(3,-1,-2)} & =\\frac{(3)(-1)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+4(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{9+16}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19f4fbac-4061-5f3d-9799-55a99d06ca9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266362} {:answer/id #uuid "399da875-0cc4-5b61-a973-cc4519bb7617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266651} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0c297e-304e-5779-afcf-cf59460c8afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267252} {:answer/id #uuid "ad65bdc8-68c1-535f-a56b-c476a9c4b5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267709} {:answer/id #uuid "f1057cc8-fca6-5ace-b14b-ab32a6cdb93e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268287} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e32f98a-0b5b-5df5-ace1-4bb6116c03b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bd6a5391-fcf3-5a3e-bf4c-ad2c6e4a6452" :question/math-academy-id "q-1341" :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨1,-2,4⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨11,4,-1⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{a}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{b}=$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{a}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 11+(-2)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =11+(-8)+(-4) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6896d0d5-0d79-5756-b49d-54e6544b747c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267074} {:answer/id #uuid "739434d2-a34f-51ba-85b2-ecb051fb40b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267159} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9709bd-77ac-5c62-901d-3e693169f7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267686} {:answer/id #uuid "d662411b-1715-5ff4-8a35-4927cc146fdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268054} {:answer/id #uuid "d927e205-8536-566a-9f6c-22e1afa9f648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186267159} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6039b6c-4aa0-5bb3-9ed0-9729597efec4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "214450de-6c4e-5468-90b3-b289da0c12f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-1348" :question/problem "Let $p=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ n\\end{bmatrix}$ and $q=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that $p⊥q$, find the value of $n.$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $p⊥q$ ($p$ and $q$ are perpendicular) if and only if $p\\cdot q=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+n\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =28-4n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 28-4n & =0 \\\\ 28 & =4n \\\\ n & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275151 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0532f827-efa9-5943-921e-cd8747195164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272435} {:answer/id #uuid "a34610b7-078b-5a23-bfdf-b83fba68fb35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273796} {:answer/id #uuid "aa05f3bb-4c79-5366-99b2-4194d18ca74e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273855} {:answer/id #uuid "ad550bfa-33c2-5773-873f-42e48064e922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273882} {:answer/id #uuid "c15bdd0d-cf56-529b-ba9a-ea8d3c6be943" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274066}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273882} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eeb4dbf-b5b3-5475-acd4-8a11aa7e8123" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "633e79f5-4963-57db-9237-badf9fbf96a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-14449" :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 5x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =5x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+5\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06faa5e0-4af8-5348-9efd-11165c5b749d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272448} {:answer/id #uuid "316a580c-b1ad-519a-9924-7acafc208775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272829} {:answer/id #uuid "515140df-a38b-5675-b2b8-0083349b8513" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{cos}⁡6x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273080} {:answer/id #uuid "632167df-25c2-51dc-8e43-73f4595dd519" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273239} {:answer/id #uuid "e8961a49-7238-5263-89a6-7dc02e214375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274443} :answer-field/id #uuid "00d03065-2e89-51a1-b42e-23e110a6964b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "efa8bb83-66d2-57ab-8d51-71c6b76f0e3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-14453" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=6x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 6x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 6\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)-6(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))+C \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b799c33-4069-5d75-9acf-2c1fb425b3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272775} {:answer/id #uuid "5eec3c9c-1f5e-50d8-ad72-41bdf8012479" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273202} {:answer/id #uuid "8a45bf0f-9d2b-5f0b-8d67-58b93e277d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273578} {:answer/id #uuid "9885a09f-ec2b-514c-a625-30bef7ad1a3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273711} {:answer/id #uuid "d1aedd3b-9487-5b78-b830-5dd393643e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274218}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274218} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac0269b0-7a87-54a9-bbbd-f720ed0c881a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba25d72c-66e5-5660-8cd6-858347d6e9a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-149714" :question/problem "![](images/28/2860103dc3d1019cddcffa504b3cce1fb8a84cfd046bc796712d1e16516d5269.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,e),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,⟹\\,x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t⟩,\\,1\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4y{e}^{2x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4y{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t} \\\\  & =4t({t}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(4{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b6c179-73bb-5998-9a66-e7ee5c7354ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272594} {:answer/id #uuid "31820ed3-8684-5823-b207-558c302546c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272830} {:answer/id #uuid "48925fc8-d494-54fd-9553-6cb85067e122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273007} {:answer/id #uuid "604802b6-34cb-5971-9a07-92a58988eaf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273218} {:answer/id #uuid "70fe7ed9-a4bf-5385-8fdc-2d4b842e7f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273007} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e949706-b44d-5eac-8496-453a3fc3a2e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9463919f-5148-546d-ad7d-95a446b32afe" :question/math-academy-id "q-149727" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d060e257d74e6e0e6b7f35bfb89e9e974499059ddf4d029c0cace195ba67c13.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2y-4$ from $(0,2)$ to $(2,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2t-4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t-4,\\,t⟩,\\,2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(2t-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4-t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "179c557a-d0d0-5ebb-811c-2baf6a3cefd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{6}-6\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272593} {:answer/id #uuid "781b70bc-38cd-5810-ad6e-167d9400e156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273410} {:answer/id #uuid "d73b4363-3c1d-563d-8b69-1e8275c1c9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274269} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3eabed-9398-5497-8cc2-1d7f384966fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274495} {:answer/id #uuid "f863851f-99c9-59c9-9adb-eb61d4881fa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274565}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274269} :answer-field/id #uuid "c355ca0d-7553-591c-b07c-62d0f6a0048b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b09217dd-ddfe-59be-95cc-af28d1d6d361" :question/math-academy-id "q-149745" :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x={e}^{y/2}$ from $(1,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={e}^{t/2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{e}^{t/2},\\,t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t/2})i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\,i+j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{t}}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y} \\\\  & =6{({e}^{t/2})}^{6}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}6{e}^{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={e}^{t}+4,$ $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2({6}^{3/2}-{5}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(6\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275256 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32d3c794-0fd5-59cf-aa3b-e2dc7aaf04de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464402} {:answer/id #uuid "13f3080f-2a2e-5f68-ad93-0dc5cf51e7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464403}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464402} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd59685b-e36e-57fc-b0de-87a808e2950e" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465135} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3a5d282-29ec-5062-816b-49f4eab07dc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464404} {:answer/id #uuid "058f6b84-807f-5c96-abd5-f5b7e248e1d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464405}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464405} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f03e31d-0bb7-55ed-a6f4-3f59148d1089" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465136} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba0d7311-7b93-5e4b-a814-aa12d234796a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464406} {:answer/id #uuid "45d3662e-9e38-5c21-9f50-1fdddab0e3a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464407} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a145f94-84f0-55f4-9b95-b1c537573877" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "08ceb92c-03d7-50b0-a12d-3ba81bdd7b99" :question/math-academy-id "q-150011" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d2aa60719643235f5ae684d59607199731626e15bac40fd6b14d071c099ab4a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b8ef08638551571caa43150080a8550a0b61ed24eaa7c6da73fe66edd161e8e.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/28/28db5c349d3455c58f81da7c181586ba4e0ad5662f35faa2575d6622b3713988.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}]}
